WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.319 --> 00:00:02.867
- If you could redo anything
about your twenties--

00:00:02.867 --> 00:00:04.402
- Oh my God.

00:00:04.402 --> 00:00:05.568
- What would it be?

00:00:05.568 --> 00:00:07.271
Who would he be? (laughs)

00:00:07.271 --> 00:00:09.007
- I was just trying to pick which one.

00:00:09.007 --> 00:00:10.022
- I was joking.

00:00:10.022 --> 00:00:11.246
- No literally.

00:00:11.246 --> 00:00:12.904
- I was like, which one?

00:00:12.904 --> 00:00:14.671
- Cause that would be my redo.

00:00:14.671 --> 00:00:15.834
- I'm Gwyneth Paltrow,

00:00:15.834 --> 00:00:19.434
and here we are for
BuzzFeed with Jessica Alba.

00:00:19.434 --> 00:00:21.674
- We're giving life advice.

00:00:21.674 --> 00:00:24.274
You're gonna live by all of this advice.

00:00:24.274 --> 00:00:25.878
- How do you start off each morning?

00:00:25.878 --> 00:00:27.612
I am curious about this.

00:00:27.612 --> 00:00:29.234
- (laughs) Really?

00:00:29.234 --> 00:00:30.067
- [Gwyneth] Yeah.

00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:32.003
- I have two different
starts of the morning,

00:00:32.003 --> 00:00:34.955
cause I go through phases of exercising

00:00:34.955 --> 00:00:36.876
and phases of not exercising.

00:00:36.876 --> 00:00:40.152
So in my phases of exercising
is I wake up early,

00:00:40.152 --> 00:00:44.198
and I go to the gym when
I'm still blurry-eyed.

00:00:44.198 --> 00:00:46.914
But I always take like, a
tea, so I'm drinking that

00:00:46.914 --> 00:00:49.015
to try and wake up on the way to the gym.

00:00:49.015 --> 00:00:50.335
I sweat it out for 45 minutes,

00:00:50.335 --> 00:00:52.618
I come home by the time
the kids are waking up.

00:00:52.618 --> 00:00:54.017
- Wow. That's commitment.

00:00:54.017 --> 00:00:54.938
- That's the way.

00:00:54.938 --> 00:00:58.486
It's early, but that's the
only way I can see them,

00:00:58.486 --> 00:00:59.810
then go to work and do other things.

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:03.977
- How long do your workout
phases last before you lapse?

00:01:05.965 --> 00:01:06.996
- Sometimes it's a few weeks,

00:01:06.996 --> 00:01:10.020
sometimes it's a few
months actually, yeah.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:13.620
How do you motivate yourself to work out

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:16.037
after a long, exhausting day?

00:01:17.059 --> 00:01:18.687
- When I first met the trainer

00:01:18.687 --> 00:01:20.823
that I've been with forever and ever,

00:01:20.823 --> 00:01:24.282
I had just had my son like
maybe 10 months before,

00:01:24.282 --> 00:01:26.553
and I could not lose
the last bit of weight,

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:30.421
and it was very demoralizing,
and I wasn't seeing change.

00:01:30.421 --> 00:01:32.961
And I said to her one morning, like,

00:01:32.961 --> 00:01:35.102
"I really really don't wanna do this.

00:01:35.102 --> 00:01:37.606
"Like, I can't, I don't want to."

00:01:37.606 --> 00:01:41.773
And she said to me, "Stop
giving yourself that option."

00:01:43.032 --> 00:01:45.125
She's like, "Do you say to yourself like,

00:01:45.125 --> 00:01:46.597
"I don't wanna brush my teeth?"

00:01:46.597 --> 00:01:47.916
She's like, "You just brush them."

00:01:47.916 --> 00:01:50.627
And she's like, "You are just doing this."

00:01:50.627 --> 00:01:53.476
And it kind of changed the
way that I perceived it,

00:01:53.476 --> 00:01:55.968
so I took the optionality out of it,

00:01:55.968 --> 00:01:57.413
and I was like, "I just do it."

00:01:57.413 --> 00:01:59.883
And then it wasn't like, ugh,

00:01:59.883 --> 00:02:02.046
like rolling a boulder up a hill.

00:02:02.046 --> 00:02:03.229
- It wasn't an option.

00:02:03.229 --> 00:02:04.239
- Wasn't an option.

00:02:04.239 --> 00:02:05.258
- It's just a part of your life.

00:02:05.258 --> 00:02:06.974
- It's like, you brush your
teeth, you take a shower,

00:02:06.974 --> 00:02:10.541
you work out, like it's not,
"I don't feel like it today."

00:02:10.541 --> 00:02:13.752
What is one beauty secret or
tip that's changed your life?

00:02:13.752 --> 00:02:18.595
- When I dye my hair, which
isn't often, but when I do,

00:02:18.595 --> 00:02:21.653
I sleep with oil and leave-in conditioner

00:02:21.653 --> 00:02:23.884
in my hair most nights.

00:02:23.884 --> 00:02:26.289
- Oh, before you dye or after?

00:02:26.289 --> 00:02:27.275
- [Jessica] After.

00:02:27.275 --> 00:02:28.645
- I'm gonna do that.

00:02:28.645 --> 00:02:33.611
- What's one thing every woman
should do in their twenties?

00:02:33.611 --> 00:02:34.800
- I feel like every woman should

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.253
live in Manhattan in their twenties.

00:02:37.253 --> 00:02:39.298
- That's a really, really
good recommendation.

00:02:39.298 --> 00:02:41.600
- Even if it's just a short time.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.242
But I think it's a great thing to do.

00:02:43.242 --> 00:02:46.825
I would've loved to
have the opportunity to

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:51.957
kind of start my self-exploration
earlier, in my twenties.

00:02:52.846 --> 00:02:55.827
I feel like I really had my head up my ass

00:02:55.827 --> 00:02:57.804
until I was about 38.

00:02:57.804 --> 00:03:00.330
Do you believe in soulmates?

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:04.105
And if you do believe in
soulmates, how do you imagine.

00:03:04.105 --> 00:03:05.338
Do you believe in soulmates?

00:03:05.338 --> 00:03:08.734
- It's weird, you know,
I did when I was younger,

00:03:08.734 --> 00:03:11.491
and then I got really, I don't know,

00:03:11.491 --> 00:03:13.765
I just didn't after my first breakup.

00:03:13.765 --> 00:03:17.140
And then when I met my husband,

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:21.124
I felt like I had known him my
whole life the day I met him.

00:03:21.124 --> 00:03:23.848
He was very familiar,
and he felt like family.

00:03:23.848 --> 00:03:27.090
What's the best piece of
advice you've ever been given

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:29.716
that you keep with you to this day?

00:03:29.716 --> 00:03:32.310
- My dad gave me the best advice like,

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:35.013
pertaining to every situation.

00:03:35.013 --> 00:03:36.744
In my twenties I was having trouble

00:03:36.744 --> 00:03:38.581
with one of my girlfriends who I'd,

00:03:38.581 --> 00:03:40.241
you know, known since grade school.

00:03:40.241 --> 00:03:42.921
And he said, "You can't
make new old friends."

00:03:42.921 --> 00:03:44.414
And it really stuck with me.

00:03:44.414 --> 00:03:45.980
That's actually a piece of advice

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:47.602
I think you can apply to other things too.

00:03:47.602 --> 00:03:49.694
But it's like the value in time.

00:03:49.694 --> 00:03:52.237
You know, it's sort of like
what you were saying with Cash,

00:03:52.237 --> 00:03:53.922
like the value that's created

00:03:53.922 --> 00:03:55.462
when two people make a commitment,

00:03:55.462 --> 00:03:57.286
whether it's a relationship,
or a friendship,

00:03:57.286 --> 00:03:59.550
or a parent-child relationship.

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:02.538
To commit to the commitment
is very powerful.

00:04:02.538 --> 00:04:04.180
- It is very powerful.

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:08.245
I think people who come
from my kind of background

00:04:08.245 --> 00:04:11.725
feel like certain things
aren't meant for you

00:04:11.725 --> 00:04:15.455
because your circumstance is different.

00:04:15.455 --> 00:04:16.965
And my mom was always like,

00:04:16.965 --> 00:04:18.656
"You can have absolutely anything

00:04:18.656 --> 00:04:21.219
"you want in this world if you work hard."

00:04:21.219 --> 00:04:22.052
- It's true.

00:04:22.052 --> 00:04:24.547
- And so that stuck with me from day one.

00:04:24.547 --> 00:04:25.462
- She must be so proud of you.

00:04:25.462 --> 00:04:26.661
- Yeah.

00:04:26.661 --> 00:04:28.321
- I feel like I always learn something

00:04:28.321 --> 00:04:29.693
when I sit next to her.

00:04:29.693 --> 00:04:31.036
- I know, me too.

00:04:31.036 --> 00:04:34.347
- Like, you always are
surprising in the best way

00:04:34.347 --> 00:04:35.525
with your answers.

00:04:35.525 --> 00:04:38.548
It was nice to hear
about what you just said

00:04:38.548 --> 00:04:40.721
with your mom and your family.

00:04:40.721 --> 00:04:43.795
- You know what I think is
great about the show that we did

00:04:43.795 --> 00:04:47.516
is A, we're not reality
TV folks, obviously.

00:04:47.516 --> 00:04:50.134
People get to see a side to Gwyneth,

00:04:50.134 --> 00:04:54.132
and I got to see and learn a side to her,

00:04:54.132 --> 00:04:57.265
that she's so freaking smart,

00:04:57.265 --> 00:04:59.913
but so emotionally intelligent.

00:04:59.913 --> 00:05:03.426
And I think that's something
that you can't really learn,

00:05:03.426 --> 00:05:05.030
you either have it or you don't.

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:06.864
I haven't seen a lot of actresses

00:05:06.864 --> 00:05:09.999
that have been emotionally
intelligent in this way,

00:05:09.999 --> 00:05:11.862
and I really appreciate it.

00:05:11.862 --> 00:05:13.495
- Thank you.

00:05:13.495 --> 00:05:14.328
- It's cool.

00:05:14.328 --> 00:05:16.273
- That is so nice. Thank you.

00:05:16.273 --> 00:05:18.692
Anyway, I need to go
on BuzzFeed, in fact--

00:05:18.692 --> 00:05:19.738
- [Jessica] It's really fun.

00:05:19.738 --> 00:05:21.425
- [Gwyneth] My son yesterday
was doing something

00:05:21.425 --> 00:05:22.718
that he learned on BuzzFeed.

00:05:22.718 --> 00:05:25.718
I don't remember what it was though.

